<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kilwa Kisiwani</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kilwa Kisiwani is an island off the southern coast of Tanzania, seat of the Kilwa Sultanate, which from the 13th century controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1331 and praised it as one of the most beautiful towns in the world. The Portuguese sacked it in 1505, and its ruins, including the Great Mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a>, form a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="Kilwa Kisiwani" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khalidsalewa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_Kilwa_Interior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
